(Motzkin, Belaga,\nL13@P0: Pan).\nL14@P0: In characteristic 2, for every n > 1 and every finite field of size at least 2n, we\nL15@P0: prove that an n-multiplication chain cannot parametrise all value vectors at 2n distinct\nL16@P0: evaluation points, even with arbitrary preprocessing. We give ⌊n/2⌋+1 multiplication\nL17@P0: schedules over characteristic 2, each with an explicit inverse, for every odd degree\nL18@P0: n ≤ 21 and conjecture that this is possible for all n.\nL19@P0: We also give an injective polynomial construction for universal hashing that uses\nL38@P1: The trick is to allow some preprocessing of the coefficients a0, . . . , an−1. For example,\nL39@P1: the polynomial\nL40@P1: P = 9 + 7x + 5x\nL41@P1: 2 + 3x3 + x4\nL42@P1: can be evaluated by first computing y = x(x + 1) and then P = (x + y − 1)(y + 4) + 13\nL43@P1: using just two multiplications rather than three with Horner.\nL44@P1: In the 50s Motzkin [Mot55] showed that, after preprocessing the coefficients, any monic\nL45@P1: polynomial of degree n can be evaluated using just ⌈n/2⌉ multiplications (Belaga [Bel58],\nL46@P1: see scheme (0.5) in Pan’s survey [Pan66]; Knuth’s formulation of the method [Knu97] uses\nL47@P1: ⌊n/2⌋ + 2 multiplications for a general polynomial). The downside is that the method\nL48@P1: requires solving a polynomial system of equations over the complex numbers, which re\u0002duces precision and breaks over finite fields. Many other methods have been proposed, but\nL49@P1: rational preprocessing was known only in special cases—Pan’s sextic scheme [Pan61] and\nL50@P1: individual polynomials for which the preprocessing systems of Motzkin and Belaga [Bel58]\nL51@P1: happen to have rational solutions, such as the quartic above—until Winograd, and Rabin\nL52@P1: and Winograd [RW72], gave a general way to preprocess the coefficients using rational op\u0002erations (Section 2.4). Unfortunately, their method requires n/2+2⌈log n⌉ multiplications,\nL53@P1: and they suspected that this logarithmic overhead might be necessary.\nL59@P1: magnitude, but this seems to be a hard problem.\nL60@P1: In this paper we answer Rabin and Winograd’s conjecture in the negative by showing\nL61@P1: how to evaluate any monic polynomial of degree n using just ⌊n/2⌋+1 multiplications with\nL62@P1: rational preprocessing. Their question had two halves, the O(log n) excess multiplications\nL63@P1: and the n+o(n) additions; we settle the first and pay on the second, using 5\nL64@P1: 4\nL65@P1: n+O((log n)\nL66@P1: 2\nL67@P1: )\nL68@P1: additions rather than n + o(n), and whether ⌊n/2⌋ + 1 multiplications can be combined\nL69@P1: with n + o(n) additions is open. The addition count matches the 5\nL70@P1: 4\nL71@P1: n of Winograd’s\nL72@P1: 1970 monic construction (Knuth’s exercise 44 [Knu97, §4.6.4]), the precursor of Rabin–\nL73@P1: Winograd’s, which later reduced the additions to (1 + ε)n at the same 1\nL74@P1: 2\nL75@P1: n + O(log n)\nL76@P1: multiplications: the constant 5\nL77@P1: 4\nL78@P1: survived our change of construction, while the logarithmic\nL79@P1: excess of multiplications did not. At odd degrees the multiplication count is optimal for\nL80@P1: monic polynomials under any preprocessing whatsoever, by Motzkin’s bound (for a general\nL81@P1: polynomial of odd degree n ≥ 9 our ⌊n/2⌋ + 2 is one more than the complex optimum of\nL82@P1: 1Although named after William George Horner, this method is much older, as it has been attributed\nL83@P1: to Joseph-Louis Lagrange by Horner himself, and can be traced back many hundreds of years to Chinese\nL84@P1: and Persian mathematicians; see, e.g., Knuth [Knu62].\nL85@P1-2: 2Revah and Pan [Rev75, Pan78]); at even degrees it is one more than the algebraic optimum,\nL86@P2: and we prove at degree 6 that the extra multiplication is necessary once the rational (single\u0002valued) preprocessing must have no exceptional inputs, conjecturing the same at every\nL87@P2: even degree at least 6. The conjecture is specific to single-valued rational preprocessing:\nL88@P2: for algebraic preprocessing Pan conjectures the opposite [Pan78] (Section 6). With fixed\nL89@P2: scalar multiplications free, the same schedules use at most 2n additions or subtractions\nL90@P2: uniformly and at most 5\nL91@P2: 4\nL92@P2: n + O((log n)\nL93@P2: 2\nL94@P2: ) asymptotically. The leading constant is therefore\nL95@P2: within 25% of the classical n-addition lower bound [Bel58, Bel61], a comparison across\nL96@P2: cost models: the classical count has no free constant multiples, and synthesising ours by\nL97@P2: doubling costs about 4% more additions (Section 6).\nL98@P2: Main Results. Section 2 formalizes our model (arithmetic circuits / straight-line pro\u0002grams) and the notion of rational preprocessing/decoding. Our main results are:\nL99@P2: • Upper bound. For every n ≥ 1 and every field of characteristic zero, or of charac\u0002teristic p > n, there is an evaluation scheme for monic degree-n polynomials that uses\nL100@P2: ⌊n/2⌋+ 1 multiplications and admits rational preprocessing. Its addition count is at\nL101@P2: most min{2n, 5\nL102@P2: 4\nL103@P2: n+ 6⌈log2 n⌉\nL111@P2: fixed-circuit bound holds in general.\nL112@P2: • Lower bound. Motzkin’s bound shows that monic polynomials of degree n need\nL113@P2: ⌈n/2⌉ multiplications under any preprocessing, so our odd-degree schemes are op\u0002timal for monic polynomials. For even degrees we prove that degree-6 evaluation with\nL114@P2: everywhere-defined rational decoding requires at least 4 multiplications (Section 4)—\nL115@P2: one more than Pan’s rational sextic scheme, which is undefined on a hypersurface—\nL133@P3: 2n at 2n points, for any n > 1 and\nL134@P3: whatever preprocessing of the parameters is allowed—affine, rational, or arbitrary.\nL135@P3: Unlike Section 4 this covers every n, and assumes nothing about the degree.\nL136@P3: • Experiments. Fast evaluation of small low-degree polynomials is important in\nL137@P3: many applications, such as 