about everywhere-defined preprocessing. A\nL181@P4: right-inverse alone does not imply injectivity. Our constructions prove more: their explicit\nL182@P4: decoder is a polynomial left-inverse of coeff. Since the parameter and coefficient spaces\nL183@P4: both have dimension n, the algebraic argument in Lemma 11 makes the two inverses agree;\nL184@P4: the coefficient map is in fact a polynomial automorphism.\nL185@P4: Hashing viewpoint. If we sample θ at random and evaluate E(x; θ) at an input x,\nL186@P4: we obtain a polynomial-hash family. For the coefficient maps constructed here, distinct\nL187@P4: parameters induce distinct polynomials and every monic coefficient vector occurs. This\nL188@P4: two-sided property—which is stronger than merely having a rational section—is what lets\nL189@P4: preprocessing be interpreted as selecting a polynomial from a well-defined family.\nL190@P4: 2.1 Main Result\nL191@P4: Our main contribution is an evaluation scheme with essentially the minimum possible\nL192@P4: number of multiplications while still admitting rational preprocessing.\nL193@P4: Theorem 1. For every n ≥ 1 and every field F of characteristic zero, or of characteristic\nL194@P4: p > n, there is an evaluation scheme for monic degree-n polynomials over F that uses\nL195@P4: ⌊n/2⌋ + 1 multiplications, admits rational preprocessing, and uses at most\nL196@P4: min \u001a2n,\nL197@P4: 5\nL198@P4: 4\nL199@P4: n + 6⌈log2 n⌉\nL200@P4: 2 + 1\u001b\nL201@P4: additions or subtractions, and can be arranged with multiplicative height at most\nL202@P4: 2⌈log2 n⌉ + 4 for odd n and 2⌈log2 n⌉ + 5 for even n (Theorem 2).\nL203@P4: Remark 1 (Positive characteristic). The condition p > n is a convenient sufficient con\u0002dition, not a sharp one. The decoder divides only by explicitly displayed integer pivots,\nL204@P4: and every prime divisor of those pivots is at most n. Thus the same construction works\nL205@P4: in any characteristic in which the pivots occurring in the degree-n re