lationships. The two-sided lemma cited above\nL235@P5: then turns that decoder into the required preprocessing map.\nL236@P5: A warm-up: degree 3 with two multiplications. Let n = 3. Compute H2(x) = x\nL237@P5: 2\nL371@P7: • Rabin–Winograd preprocessing. Rabin and Winograd [RW72] showed that,\nL372@P7: with preprocessing of the coefficient vector c using only rational operations, one can\nL373@P7: evaluate a degree-k polynomial with about k/2 + O(log k) multiplications. Their\nL374@P7: work framed preprocessing in a way that is compatible with finite fields, and they\nL375@P7: conjectured their overhead beyond k/2 was essentially necessary.\nL376@P7-8: 8Our setting: preprocessing as a parameterization. In this paper, we consider an\nL401@P8: a bijection over every field of the stated characteristic, finite fields included. Operationally\nL402@P8: this gives two benefits:\nL403@P8: • Soundness of preprocessing. Given a uniformly random coefficient vector c (the\nL404@P8: standard k-wise key), we can compute parameters θ = pre(c) such that E(x; θ) ≡\nL405@P8: hc(x). This lets us implement k-wise hashing using the fast circuit while preserving\nL406@P8: the exact algebraic distribution that the usual Vandermonde proof assumes.\nL407@P8: • “Sample-θ” viewpoint. Since coeff is a bijection of F\nL408@P8: k\nL409@P8: , a uniform θ ∈ F\nL410@P8: k\nL411@P8: induces\nL412@P8: a uniform monic coefficient vector, exactly; over a finite field this is precisely the\nL413@P8: distribution the Vandermonde argument needs, with no generic or exceptional-set\nL414@P8: qualification. (A map that is only generically injective would leave a bias on its\nL415@P8: exceptional set, which over a finite field is not negligible; Lemma 11 is what makes\nL416@P8: the exact statement available.)\nL417@P8: This is the sense in which “fast evaluation with rational preprocessing” becomes a biject\u0002ive/injective parameterization problem.\nL418@P8: Implication of Theorem 1 for k-wise hashing. Applying Theorem 1 with n = k,\nL419@P8: we obtain an evaluation scheme for (1) that uses only ⌊k/2⌋ + 1 multiplications after\nL420@P8: preprocessing the coefficients into runtime parameters. This improves over Horner’s k −\nL421@P8: 1 multiplications and removes the O(log k) overhead in Rabin–Winograd while staying\nL422@P8: within the rational preprocessing model. We evaluate practical performance trade-offs\nL423@P8: against Horner, Estrin-style evaluation, and Rabin–Winograd in Section 5.2.\nL424@P8-9: 92.4 Related Work\nL425@P9: Polynomial evaluation with preprocessing. Horner’s rule evaluates a degree-n poly\u0002nomial with n multiplications, and Pan showed that without preprocessing this is op\u0002timal [Pan66]. Motzkin [Mot55] introduced preconditioning: if the same polynomial\nL426@P9: is evaluated at many points, the coefficients may be transformed for free in advance.\nL427@P9: Motzkin [Mot55] and Belaga [Bel58, Bel61] proved that even with preconditioning ⌊n/2⌋+1\nL428@P9: multiplications and n additions are necessary for a general polynomial of degree n ≥ 2\nL429@P9: (Theorems M, A and C in Knuth [Knu97, §4.6.4]; Belaga’s note states the multiplicat\u0002ive bound as ⌈n/2⌉, and Pan [Pan66] sharpened it to ⌊n/2⌋ + 1); Motzkin, Belaga, Eve\nL430@P9: and Knuth [Mot55, Bel58, Eve64, Knu62] gave schemes with ⌈n/2⌉ + 1 multiplications\nL431@P9: for a general polynomial of degree n, that is ⌈n/2⌉ for a monic one (Belaga’s Theorem 1,\nL432@P9: displayed as scheme (0.5) and Theorem 3.2 of Pan’s survey [Pan66]; Knuth’s Theorem E\nL433@P9: states the count as ⌊n/2⌋ + 2, one more when n is even), but their preprocessing re\u0002quires finding roots of polynomials, so the transformed coefficients generally live in an\nL434@P9: algebraic extension of the field generated by the coefficients (a real extension when the\nL435@P9: coefficients are real, in Pan’s scheme (0.7) [Pan66], Eve’s variant [Eve64] and Knuth’s\nL436@P9: Theorem E). These schemes attain the multiplicative lower bound for even n; for odd\nL437@P9: n ≥ 9 it is attained with complex parameters by Revah [Rev75] and Pan [Pan78]. Belaga\nL438@P9: already noted that the preprocessing system of his Theorem 1 is in general not solvable\nL439@P9: over the reals [Bel58]; the real-valued variants just cited fill that gap with algebraic pre\u0002processing, and Theorem 1 supplies rational, everywhere-defined preprocessing instead,\nL440@P9: reaching the multiplication count of Knuth’s Theorem E [Knu62, Knu97] (⌊n/2⌋ + 2 for a\nL441@P9: general polynomial) without root-finding and without the shift y = x + c that Theorem E\nL442@P9: requires, although not its n additions. Pan [Pan61] also gave a scheme for sextics with\nL443@P9: three multiplications whose preprocessing is rational but undefined on a hypersurface of\nL444@P9: coefficient vectors; we return to it in Section 4. Pan’s survey [Pan66] and the monograph of\nL445@P9: B¨urgisser, Clausen and Shokrollahi [BCS97, Chapter 5] give the classical picture, includ\u0002ing the transcendence-degree argument behind the lower bounds. Rabin and Winograd\nL446@P9: introduced rational preprocessing [RW72], where preprocessing uses only field operations\nL447@P9: and division, obtaining n/2+O(log n) multiplications; the O(log n) term pays for comput\u0002ing x\nL448@P9: 2\nL449@P9: , x4, . . . , x2\nL450@P9: ⌊log n⌋\nL451@P9: by repeated squaring. Knuth’s exercise 44 [Knu97, §4.6.4] records\nL452@P9: Winograd’s 1970 precursor of this construction for monic polynomials: 1\nL453@P9: 2\nL454@P9: n + ⌊log2 n⌋ − 1\nL455@P9: multiplications beyond the ⌊log2 n⌋ squarings and at most 5\nL456@P9: 4\nL457@P9: n additions, with parameters\nL458@P9: that are integer polynomials in the coefficients and hence valid modulo every m. Theorem 1\nL459@P9: removes the logarithmic term at the same leading addition constant 5\nL460@P9: 4\nL461@P9: , but its decoder\nL462@P9: divides by the integer pivots of Remark 1, so it is not valid modulo every m. Pan’s\nL463@P9: resolution of the complex case [Pan78] explicitly sets aside evaluation over the rational\nL464@P9: numbers, referring to Rabin and Winograd; Theorem 1 settles the multiplication count in\nL465@P9: that setting for monic polynomials of odd degree (for general polynomials of odd degree\nL466@P9: n ≥ 9 it is within one of the complex optimum of Revah and Pan). A general-purpose\nL467@P9: method that instead minimises non-scalar multiplications is Paterson–Stockmeyer [PS73],\nL468@P9: and Kedlaya and Umans [KU11] gave asymptotically fast algorithms with preprocessing.\nL469@P9: In cryptography, Bernstein’s BRW polynomials [Ber07] specialise the Rabin–Winograd\nL470@P9: recursion to message authentication: the message blocks become the coefficients and the\nL475@P10: parameter vector corresponds to a uniformly random monic polynomial and the scheme\nL476@P10: yields k-wise independent hashing. BRW is only injective: distinct messages give distinct\nL477@P10: polynomials, but not every polynomial arises, which suffices for almost-universal hashing\nL478@P10: (an “authenticator”) but not for k-independence. Our main construction is of the first\nL479@P10: kind: it keeps a bijective rational preprocessing and removes the logarithmic number of\nL1068@P21: For odd n this equals the ⌊n/2⌋ + 1 of Theorem 1, so the odd-degree schedules of this\nL1069@P21: paper are optimal outright. For even n = 2m the bound is m, and it is attained: by\nL1070@P21: Motzkin and Belaga [Bel58, Pan66] with algebraic preprocessing, and at n = 6 even with\nL1071@P21: rational preprocessing. Pan’s scheme [Pan61] (displayed as (16) in [Knu97, §4.6.4])\nL1072@P21: z = (x + α0)x + α1, w = z + x + α2, P =\nL1073@P21: \nL1074@P21: (z − x + α3) w + α4\nL1075@P21: \u0001\nL1076@P21: z + α5\nL1793@P36: , which fails in characteristic 2 since 2ab = 0. Does one fixed circuit\nL1794@P36: topology with rational preprocessing achieve ⌊n/2⌋+ 1 multiplications in characteristic 2?\nL1795@P36: We currently give such fixed constructions, with explicit inverses, only at odd degrees up\nL1796@P36: to 21 (Section A; Section J for degree 5).\nL1797@P36: Exceptional inputs at even degree. Pan’s sextic scheme (Section 4) reaches\nL1798@P36: Motzkin’s bound of n/2 multiplications at n = 6 with rational preprocessing that\nL1799@P36: is undefined on a hypersurface, and Section 4 shows that no single-valued rational\nL1800@P36: preprocessing removes this hypersurface. Does a generically rational scheme with n/2\nL1801@P36: multiplications exist for every even n ≥ 8? Pan [Pan78] credits Pan (1961) [Pan61] with a\nL1802@P36: real scheme for n = 8 with (n + 2)/2 multiplications, that is n/2 for a monic polynomial,\nL1803@P36: and n + 2 additions for almost all polynomials; we could not determine whether its\nL1804@P36: preprocessing is rational, as it is at n = 6. And is the exceptional set always unavoidable\nL1805@P36: for rational (single-valued) preprocessing, as we conjecture? For algebraic preprocessing\nL1806@P36: Pan conjectures the opposite, that the exceptional sets of his schemes can be removed\nL1807@P36: at every degree, and proves it over C for even n ≡ 2 (mod 4), n ≥ 10, at n/2 monic\nL1808@P36: multiplications and n additions [Pan78].\nL1809@P36: Lower bounds for characteristic 2. The proof in Section 4 uses properties specific\nL1810@P36: to fields of characteristic ̸= 2, but a different argument applies there: over a finite field\nL1811@P36: of characteristic 2 with at least 2n elements, Theorem 7 rules out n multiplications for\nL1812@P36: bijective evaluation at 2n points at every n > 1, for every preprocessing map. Two\nL1813@P36: questions remain. Does the analogous bound hold for an odd number of points, that\nL1814@P36: is, for monic polynomials of odd degree? And does it hold over small fields, where the\nL1815@P36: hypothesis |F| ≥ 2n fails?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nperf(multilinear-util,bus,multi-stark,word-backend,sumcheck)!: borrowed points and one packed equality-table kernel by tcoratger · Pull Request #2305 · Plonky3/Plonky3 · GitHub (https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/pull/2305)\nciteturn8view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view1\",\"pattern\":\"cdb48a0\"}); Total lines: 433\nL378: ### Uh oh!\nL379: \nL380: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL381: \nL382: Hide details View details\nL383: \nL384: 【70†tcoratger】 merged commit 【103†`cdb48a0`】 into Plonky3:main 【104†Sep 23, 2026】\nL385: \nL386: 20 checks passed\nL387: \nL388: ### Uh oh!\nL389: \nL390: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL391: \nL392: 【105†Sign up for free】 to join this conversation on GitHub. Already have an account? 【1†Sign in to comment】\nL393: \nL394: [Input] [Input]\nL395: \nL396: ### Reviewers\nL397: \nL398: 【82†Nashtare】 Nashtare approved these changes\nL399: \nL400: [Input][Input]\nL401: \nL402: ### Assignees\nL403: \nL404: No one assigned\n"